<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<TcPlcObject Version="1.1.0.1">
  <DUT Name="ST_DeviceStateExt" Id="{5cd60f9f-dadd-0ea0-1a1b-6a46a8d3dc5a}">
    <Declaration><![CDATA[(*
    ST_DeviceStateExt - an ST_DeviceState plus a validity flag for lookups.

    PURPOSE
        Carries one device state (state name, state reference, position,
        tolerance and the beam parameters that state requires) together with
        xValid, which says whether the structure was produced by a successful
        device-state-table lookup or is a default/unloaded state. The state
        fields are inherited unchanged from ST_DeviceState; this DUT adds no
        data of its own beyond the validity flag.

    USAGE
        Return type of F_DeviceState_To_DeviceStateExt, which copies an
        ST_DeviceState and sets xValid from its i_xValid argument, and the
        declared type of the state holders in FB_LinearGovernor
        (stInitDeviceState, stTargetState, stLatchedTargetState). The producer
        sets xValid TRUE only when the state-table lookup it translated
        succeeded (stDevice.StateTable.bOk); when the lookup fails the caller
        forces xValid := FALSE after the translation.

    OBJECTIVES
        - Give consumers a documented way to tell "the requested state was
          found" from "the requested state does not exist", because a missing
          state and a found state whose position happens to be 0 are otherwise
          indistinguishable.
        - Keep ST_DeviceState free of validity handling, so code that has no
          lookup to report keeps using the base struct.
        - Default to FALSE so an instance that was never assigned never reads
          as a valid state.

    HISTORY
        2019-08-28 / Alex Wallace
            DUT added with the PMPS library migration.
        2026-11-27 / Scirix Agent
            Added this header and the xValid meaning comment; declaration
            otherwise unchanged (audit finding #245, convention
            conv_321640e095c0).

    NOTE
        An invalid structure is not blanked: on a failed lookup only xValid is
        cleared, so the inherited fields keep the values of the last successful
        lookup (or their defaults). Consumers must therefore test xValid before
        using rPosition, rTolerance or stReqBeamParam. Nothing in this library
        reads xValid today - FB_LinearGovernor writes it and reports lookup
        failures through its own xInitFault / xStateLookupError instead.
*)
TYPE ST_DeviceStateExt EXTENDS ST_DeviceState :
STRUCT
    xValid	:	BOOL := FALSE; // TRUE when the extended state structure was loaded/valid
END_STRUCT
END_TYPE
]]></Declaration>
  </DUT>
</TcPlcObject>